# O(n) time, O(n) space — n live stack frames at max depth
def sum_list_recursive(arr, i=0):
    if i == len(arr):
        return 0
    return arr[i] + sum_list_recursive(arr, i + 1)

# O(n) time, O(1) space — one frame, one variable, reused every iteration
def sum_list_iterative(arr):
    total = 0
    for x in arr:
        total += x
    return total
```

Any **linear, single-branch** recursion — one recursive call per invocation, no branching into two or more calls — can always be rewritten as a loop with an accumulator variable, dropping space from O(n) to O(1) while keeping time at O(n). This isn't always possible for recursion that branches (like naive Fibonacci or a tree traversal, where two or more calls happen per invocation) without an explicit stack or queue to replace the implicit one — but for the single-branch case, it's a mechanical, always-available conversion.
